---
import ReferenceLayout from '../../layouts/ReferenceLayout.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../lib/site.js';

const title = 'Sources and editorial method';
const description =
	'Where the text, transliterations and meanings on this site come from, and how spelling variants and diacritics are handled.';

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'WebPage',
	name: title,
	url: `${SITE_URL}/sources/`,
	inLanguage: 'en',
};
---

<ReferenceLayout title={`${title} | Kakar Adi Kali Sahasranama`} description={description} canonical="/sources/" schema={schema} colophonLabel="Sources">
	<div class="page">
		<div class="narrow">
			<nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<ol>
					<li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
					<li aria-current="page">Sources</li>
				</ol>
			</nav>

			<p class="page-kicker">Where the words come from</p>
			<h1>Sources and method</h1>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="text">
				<h2 class="sec" id="text"><span lang="sa">मूल</span> The text</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p class="drop">
						The thousand names are the Kakar Adi Kali Sahasranama, a Kali sahasranama whose names run in
						alphabetical order from the letter <em>ka</em>. The sequence, the Devanagari forms and the
						source transliterations come from the recension used to build this edition.
					</p>
					<p>
						Where a name repeats, every position is recorded on one page rather than duplicated across
						several.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="spelling">
				<h2 class="sec" id="spelling"><span lang="sa">वर्ण</span> Spelling and transliteration</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Three forms are kept for every name. The <strong>Devanagari</strong> is the original script.
						The <strong>IAST</strong> is the academic romanization with diacritics — <em>Kālī</em>, not
						<em>Kali</em>. The <strong>source transliteration</strong> is the plain-ASCII form used in the
						source corpus, which is what most readers will have seen elsewhere.
					</p>
					<p>
						Alternate spellings are listed because English renders Sanskrit inconsistently:
						<em>Kaali</em>, <em>Kalii</em> and <em>Kaalee</em> all point at the same name. Search on this
						site folds diacritics, so any of them finds the page.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="editorial">
				<h2 class="sec" id="editorial"><span lang="sa">टीका</span> Editorial</h2>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						The one-line meanings and the longer elaborations are editorial commentary written for this
						edition. They are aids to reading, not translations of a received commentary, and they carry
						no authority beyond that.
					</p>
					<p>
						Corrections are welcome. If a Devanagari form or a transliteration is wrong, say which name
						and which position.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>
		</div>
	</div>
</ReferenceLayout>
